  "badge": {
    "id": "j1-defend-the-fields",
    "name": "Defend the Fields"
  },
  "briefing": {
    "situation": "18 October 1944, 0830. Pre-invasion Leyte. Your A6M2 is at Buri with the engine running and one Zero wingman beside you. Scramble; two Ki-43 Army fighters will join after takeoff. Two American strike groups approach from the east. Keep at least three of five groups intact through the strike: the fuel farm, radio station, north hangar, south hangar, and dispersal. A group is lost if any of its tracked structures is destroyed. Cover the inbound Ki-21 until it reaches the runway-threshold area. Recover at Buri; Bayug is authorized only if Buri becomes unserviceable. Invented: this historical composite uses authored aircraft counts, positions, timing, weather, radio calls, and a reported damaged Ki-21; it does not recreate a documented engagement. The Ki-21 damage is narrative only. Buri counts as unserviceable when two of its three operations structures are destroyed: the tower, ammunition bunker, and barracks.",
    "loadout": "clean"
  },
  "history": {
    "text": "Historical composite, inspired by the Japanese defense of the Burauen airfields before the American landings; it is not a reconstruction of a documented engagement, and the exact action did not occur as authored. The Army history describes the Burauen airfields of Buri, Bayug, and San Pablo and their pre-invasion state. Buri was the most important of the three, with a runway about 5,000 feet long. This chapter places an invented defense there on 18 October 1944. Its mixed Japanese fighter cover, two American strike groups, and the damaged Ki-21 are gameplay inventions. The A6M2 Zero was outclassed by the F6F Hellcat; the task here is to break up the attacks and keep the field working.",
    "sources": [
      "U.S. Army, M. Hamlin Cannon, Leyte: The Return to the Philippines, chapter VIII (Burauen airfields and pre-invasion state): https://www.ibiblio.org/hyperwar/USA/USA-P-Return/USA-P-Return-8.html",
      "docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-14-leyte-campaign-definition.md, sections 3, 4 (J1), and 10: historical-composite designation and limits on the authored engagement"
    ]
  }
}
